From 2e26f1155c438faab1f1d4640fbbd7fb86350da8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 19:00:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] ##arch: USSE load/store fetch and increment semantics

The disassembly prints the encoded offset field of ldad/stad, as
Imagination's ref_usse_disasm does, but that field is not always the first
element accessed:
- .fetchN [b,+#k] accesses elements b+k+1 .. b+k+N (the fetch adds one
  element; the PSP2 compilers subtract it when they emit such loads).
- .fetchN [b,#k++] accesses b+k .. b+k+N-1 and then sets b += N.
- plain [b,++#k] / [b,#k++] pre/post-increment b by k.
- offsets scale by the data size (4 for ldad/stad, 2 for ldaw/staw).

memop.h holds this model for both plugins. ESIL now loads/stores every
fetched register at the right address and writes the base back
(previously a fetch read one dword at the printed offset, and ldaw was
scaled as a dword). asm.pseudo names the accessed elements, e.g.
"r3..r6 = mem[r2 + 2..5]", "r4 = mem[r0]; r0 += 1". Fetch forms with
pre-increment or negative offsets are not seen in any corpus and stay
unmodelled. Disassembly text is unchanged.

Tests: db/cmd/cmd_pseudo_usse, db/esil/usse (incl. an emulated CCB fetch).
